  {
   "id": "hmTGziI_BwAJ",
   "from": "Bob Carpenter",
   "author": "8aa9b8cc06fdc99c",
   "date": "2016-10-21T19:49:32Z",
   "subject": "Re: [stan-users] Low n_eff in measurement model",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "Another old post that nobody responded to.\r<br>\r<br>The problem is that your scale&#39;s not identified in the likelihood\r<br>only in the weak prior on tau_k.\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "\r<br>&gt;   real&lt;lower = 0&gt; tau_k;\r<br>&gt; \r<br>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "&gt;   real&lt;lower = 0&gt; tau_i[n_respondents];\r<br>\r<br>\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "&gt;   tau_i ~ normal(1, tau_i_sigma);\r<br>\r<br>&gt;   tau_k ~ normal(0, 5);  \r<br>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "\r<br>\r<br>&gt;       z[m] ~ normal(alpha[i[m]] + beta[i[m]] * theta[i[m]], tau_k * tau_i[i[m]]); \r<br>\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "I&#39;m guessing you&#39;ll see banana-shaped pairs plots in\r<br>the posterior of tau_k * tau_i.  \r<br>\r<br>This could also be vectorized for a bit more efficiency if you\r<br>could presort the conditional in the actual code out.\r<br>\r<br>I&#39;m afraid I don&#39;t have a suggestion for how to fix this.\r<br>\r<br>- Bob\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "\r<br>\r<br>\r<br>\r<br>\r<br>\r<br>&gt; On Aug 3, 2016, at 11:06 AM, Gregory &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">gregor...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; Hello everyone,\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; I have written a relatively straightforward linear model in Stan to capture a data-generating process in which users give their perceptions of the placement of a series of items on a scale. Some users are precise in their placements of the items and others are error-prone. Some items are also more difficult to place than others. I want to capture heterogeneity in individual-specific error using a multiplicative term (as done in the literature): item-specific error x individual-specific error. I have set up a model that works well with both simulated and real data without the term to capture heterogeneity in item-placement error. However, when I build on this simpler model by adding in the multiplicative term, the n_eff plummets into the single digits, and the model takes forever to fit. Does anyone have any thoughts on what might be going wrong here?\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; Thanks in advance!\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; data {\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt;   int n_items;\r<br>&gt;   int n_respondents;\r<br>&gt;   int n_obs;\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt;   int i[n_obs];  // respondent i\r<br>&gt;   int j[n_obs];  // item j\r<br>&gt;   real z[n_obs]; // item response z_ij\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; } \r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; parameters {\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt;   vector[n_respondents] alpha; // Individual-specific shift\r<br>&gt;   vector[n_respondents] beta; // Individual-specific stretch\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt;   vector[n_items] zeta_raw; // Latent &#39;true&#39; position of item\r<br>&gt;   real zeta_mu;\r<br>&gt;   real&lt;lower = 0&gt; zeta_sigma;\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt;   real&lt;lower = 0&gt; tau_j[n_items]; // Item-specific variance for placement (some items more difficult to place than others)\r<br>&gt;   real&lt;lower = 0&gt; tau_k; // Item-specific variance for a unique item in the data\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt;   real&lt;lower = 0&gt; tau_i[n_respondents]; // Individual-specific variance for item placement\r<br>&gt;   real&lt;lower = 0&gt; tau_i_sigma;\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt;   vector[n_respondents] theta; // Latent position of individual-specific unique item\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; }\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; transformed parameters {\r<br>&gt;   vector[n_items] zeta;\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt;   zeta[1] = -1; // For identification\r<br>&gt;   zeta[2] = 1;\r<br>&gt;   for(m in 3:n_items)\r<br>&gt;     zeta[m] = zeta_raw[m];\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; }\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; model {\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt;   // Priors\r<br>&gt;   alpha ~ normal(0, 10);\r<br>&gt;   beta ~ normal(0, 10);\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt;   zeta_mu ~ normal(0, 10);\r<br>&gt;   zeta_sigma ~ uniform(0, 100);\r<br>&gt;   zeta_raw ~ normal(zeta_mu, zeta_sigma);\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt;   theta ~ normal(0, 1); // For identification\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt;   tau_j ~ normal(0, 5); \r<br>&gt;   tau_k ~ normal(0, 5);  \r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt;   tau_i_sigma ~ normal(0, 5);\r<br>&gt;   tau_i ~ normal(1, tau_i_sigma);\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt;   // Likelihood\r<br>&gt;   for(m in 1:n_obs) {\r<br>&gt;     if(j[m] == (n_items + 1))\r<br>&gt;       z[m] ~ normal(alpha[i[m]] + beta[i[m]] * theta[i[m]], tau_k * tau_i[i[m]]); // Note the multiplicative error term here\r<br>&gt;     else {\r<br>&gt;       z[m] ~ normal(alpha[i[m]] + beta[i[m]] * zeta[j[m]], tau_j[j[m]] * tau_i[i[m]]); // Note the multiplicative error term here\r<br>&gt;     }\r<br>&gt;   }\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; }\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; \r<br>"
    ],
